            </HouseholdOperatingConditions>
            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="2" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="1" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage, and would benefit from some air sealing work. however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed through electrical outlets on your exterior walls, the weather stripping along the three doors, and along the baseboards (between the floor and wall). A significant amount of air leakage was also noted at your sliding patio door.



We noticed a significant amount of air leakage through the fan in your upstairs bathroom. I recommend replacing this fan with an ENERGY STARÂ® rated model.



Other upgrades recommended in this report, such as furnace upgrades and replacing your patio door with a hinged garden door, may also affect the air leakage rating for your home.  





&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $300&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;10.0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x4 studs with 4 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation to the exterior of your walls, to bring your insulation value to approximately R27. 



We typically recommend that upgrades to wall insulation be done at the same time as a siding replacement. However, since your siding appears to be in good shape and well-cared for, it may be possible to remove the siding, add insulation, and then replace the original siding instead of replacing it. If you go this route, you will need to replace the corner pieces of siding to account for the thicker walls. 





&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R15 of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $1,310&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;42.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</mainWalls>
                <foundation>

The exterior walls of your crawlspace are concrete with fibreglass batt insulation, with an estimated R value of R6. There is no framing in the crawlspace so the insulation batts are strapped to the walls. Since there is a duct blowing warm air from your furnace into the crawlspace, it is considered heated space. I have modelled an upgrade to the insulation on the exterior walls of the crawlspace. While you do have insulation on the walls, the strapping is compressing it and reducing the effective R-value of the fibreglass batts. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your current crawlspace insulation with 4 inches (R20) of XPS board insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $320&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;10.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate 100 percent of the floor and increase its insulation value by a minimum of RSI 4.2 (R-24). You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</foundation>
                <windows>

All of the windows in your living space are already triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, and appear to be in good physical condition. However, your two crawlspace windows are in poor physical condition and should be replaced. 



Should you need replace any windows because of physical damage, we recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows that are ENERGY STARÂ® qualified rated for climate zone 3. 



As your patio door is older, I have modelled replacing it with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified patio door rated for climate zone 3. I recommend a hinged garden door instead of a sliding patio door, as the hinge and locking mechanism will provide a better seal and reduce or eliminate any drafts at this door. 



I have given this a higher priority rating than my other recommended upgrades, as I noticed a significant amount of air leakage coming in around the patio door. Replacing the patio door to reduce or eliminate this air leakage will not only save you heating costs, but will also provide you with a more comfortable living environment within your home. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your patio door and crawlspace windows with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified doors and windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $208&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;6.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new patio doors or windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</windows>
                <doors>

Your front door appears to be in good shape and to have been replaced relatively recently. However, your side door is a solid wood door with a single paned window, and should be replaced. I have modelled replacing it with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified door rated for climate zone 3.



As with the patio door upgrade, this has been given a higher priority than other, more involved upgrades as it is a quick replacement that can also have an impact on your comfort. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your side door with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified door rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $70&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;2.3 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new doors from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</doors>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have an electric hot water tank and have expressed interest in replacing your wood fireplace with a propane fireplace, so I have modelled two different options for you to consider.



The first option is to keep your current hot water tank and add an R5 insulation blanket to the tank. This will reduce the amount of heat lost to the surrounding air, and lower the amount of energy required to keep the water hot. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding an R5 insulation blanket to your hot water tank, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $120&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;0.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are no rebates for this upgrade.



The second option I have modelled is to  replace your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $1,140&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, increase your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;0.5 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

As with the hot water, I have modelled two different options for you to consider. For modelling purposes, I have had to combine your two furnaces into a single furnace, and the combined capacity of the two furnaces is quite large. If you are planning to replace these furnaces, I strongly recommend speaking to an HVAC contractor to ensure that whatever you choose to replace them with is sized correctly, and to discuss your options if you would like to keep the two floors zoned separately. 



Your current oil fired furnaces were originally rated at 84.1% and 83.3% AFUE. For the first option for replacement, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency oil furnace with a minimum efficiency of 87% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency oil furnace (direct vented out a side wall), the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a correctly sized, sealed combustion oil-fired furnace with an efficiency of 92% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $1,850&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;61.3 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



Another option is to replace your furnaces with a direct vent condensing propane furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE or higher. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a correctly sized, high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $2,580&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;82.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely. The short and dark days of winter provide little or no usable energy, depending on the latitude in the NWT. However, the long hours of sunlight in spring and summer can provide usable heat and electricity for up to eight months of the year.  As prices continue to drop, solar PV panels are becoming competitive with the cost of diesel generation. 



At your request, I have modelled the addition of a solar photovoltaic system to your house. The savings presented here do not take into account shading. Since your house has a significant number of trees surrounding it, the production estimated here is likely overestimated. I strongly recommend that you consider energy efficiency upgrades before renewables. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a 3kW solar photovoltaic system, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $740&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;8.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$2,500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program. 



&lt;b&gt; GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate amount differs according to the purchase cost of the system. You would be eligible for 1/3 of the purchase cost, up to a maximum of $2,500 per year. Applicants are strongly encouraged to contact the AEA for funding pre-approval prior to installation. Projects will be funded on a first come, first serve basis until the program is fully subscribed. Projects must be installed and operational to receive the rebate. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</Renewables>
                <WaterConservation>

I recommend you replace your main floor toilet, as it is not a low flow fixture. When replacing your toilets, purchase 4.8 Liters or less, or dual-flush toilets. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing your toilet(s) with a new 4.8 Liters or less, or dual-flush toilet(s), you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save ~$50 &lt;/b&gt; per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

